  "positioning": "This is his weakest lane and the matrix says so honestly. He produces design output as part of delivering web and content work, which is real, but he is a developer and marketer who designs rather than a designer. The missing piece is not taste or tooling, it is a presented design portfolio: design roles hire on a body of work shown as design decisions, and his work is currently shown as websites and campaigns.\n\nOn current statuses most design postings grade Tier C and get dropped, which is correct. If he wants this lane to produce, the move is a portfolio, not more skills. Until then, keep it opportunistic and let lanes 7, 8 and 9 carry Track 2.",
  "interview_watch": "Expect a portfolio walkthrough and expect to be asked why, not what. Design interviews test the reasoning behind a choice. If the honest answer is that the client asked for it, say so rather than inventing a rationale, and pivot to a decision that was actually yours.",
  "pr_note": "52120 is TEER 2 and clears the floor. No RCIP list covers it, so never tag a lane 10 role RCIP. AIP covers it from a designated Atlantic employer. Ontario's Workforce Priority stream takes it with a qualifying offer.",
  "not_required": "A design degree, and formal UX credentials. A posting hard-requiring either is a credential-gate drop.",
  "notes": [
    {
      "heading": "Intro",
      "text": "NOC 52120, TEER 2. Track 2, opportunistic only. Read 00-index.md for the Status conventions and tier rules."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The standing rule for this lane",
      "text": "Do not stretch into this lane to fill a run. A role enters only where the posting is genuinely design-led and he can evidence it. This lane exists to catch the occasional real match, not to pad a thin batch, and it is the first thing to skip when a run is short."
    }
